 {:id :fork
  :title "os.fork and the forked child"
  :kind :na
  :owner "none (by design)"
  :why "pyi has no os.fork: Go's runtime is multithreaded, children start only through Go's ForkExec (PY6e: _posixsubprocess.fork_exec, posix_spawn, spawn*), so test.support's has_fork_support is False and fork tests skip ('requires working os.fork()'), subprocess's preexec_fn raises, and a test waiting for a forked child's write does not run (test/python/lib-test-skip.tsv names the one that would wait forever); the user's decision, 2026-11-04 (PY6e's DECISION (a)); since queue 278 also a test that calls os.fork itself (test_multiprocessing_fork's and test_concurrent_futures' fork start method: \"module 'os' has no attribute 'fork'\", night 12's run) and the methods of a class whose setUpClass did so, not run (named: test_multiprocessing_fork's manager classes and WithProcessesTestPool), and the fork bomb test's child (mp_fork_bomb.py fork) Queue 290 (the user's decision (a) on its DECISION, 2026-11-14): the start method forkserver, whose server process needs os.fork for each child, so its test_manager's setUpClass and test_processes' pools end in the server's EOF (night 12's 91 rows of test_multiprocessing_forkserver.test_manager and .test_processes, from :multiprocessing). The forkserver dies at its os.fork(): 3.14's default start method on Linux is forkserver, test_misc's TestStartMethod resets the start method to the default, support.reap_children reaps the dead server, and every later Process start and the modules' tearDownModule (util._cleanup_tests, forkserver._stop) hit os.waitpid(the server's pid) -> \"[Errno 10] No child process\": the three start methods' test_misc (TestSyncManagerTypes, TestWait, TestTimeouts) and concurrent_futures' forkserver initializers, by name (n-py-eval 2026-11-19: reproduced by TestStartMethod then TestWait.test_wait, which passes alone)."
