 {:id :multiprocessing
  :title "_multiprocessing"
  :kind :deferred
  :owner "PY6e"
  :why "since queue 231 _multiprocessing (SemLock, sem_unlink) and _posixshmem are provided (semaphore.c's SemLock over musl's semaphores: /dev/shm/NAME, flock and futex); since queue 249 spawn finds the parent's main module again (a script's __main__.__spec__ None, as CPython's) and its child's sys.stdin.close() works, so test_cprofile's spawn test passes. Left: the start method forkserver, whose server forks each child (os.fork: no fork in pyi, the user's decision of 2026-11-04, class :fork), test_cprofile's forkserver test; 3.14's default start method on Linux is forkserver, so a Process started without set_start_method('spawn') fails the same way (249's DECISION); night 12's run (queue 278): the forkserver's server dies on its os.fork, its client reads \"unexpected EOF\" (multiprocessing/forkserver.py read_signed; probed with Lib/test/mp_preload_main.py under pyi), so test_multiprocessing_forkserver's process tests, its preload and fork bomb tests (named) fail; since queue 286 (method_descriptor) the managers and process pools get as far as the forkserver, so its read_signed's \"unexpected EOF\" (a text only forkserver.py raises) is a pattern, the forkserver test_manager's methods are named (their setUpClass dies so), and test_concurrent_futures' tearDownModule (multiprocessing.util._cleanup_tests waits for the dead forkserver: ECHILD) Queue 290 (the user's decision (a), 2026-11-14): the forkserver test_manager's and test_processes' named rows (night 12's 91) are :fork's: the forkserver's server process forks each child."
  :decided "PY6a's split (PY6e, OS and processes); queue 200, PY6-core; queue 249; queue 278; queue 286 (the forkserver's read_signed EOF by pattern, its test_manager named, once method_descriptor let the managers start)"
  :match [#"^error unexpected EOF$" #"No module named '_multiprocessing'" #"^skipped test requires multiprocessing"]
